---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import {
  AF_PLAYBOOK_URL,
  GITHUB_PROFILE_URL,
  ISSUES_URL,
  LINKEDIN_URL,
  PARENT_URL,
  REPO_URL,
  aboutPageJsonLd,
  breadcrumbJsonLd,
  canonical,
  personJsonLd,
  withBase,
} from '../lib/seo';
import { ABOUT_PAGE } from '../lib/site-pages.js';

const url = canonical(ABOUT_PAGE.path);
const updated = '2026-10-27';
const trail = [
  { name: 'The Founder Playbook', url: canonical('/') },
  { name: 'About', url },
];
const contributing = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title={ABOUT_PAGE.title}
  description={ABOUT_PAGE.description}
  path={ABOUT_PAGE.path}
  ogType="website"
  jsonLd={[
    aboutPageJsonLd({ title: ABOUT_PAGE.title, description: ABOUT_PAGE.description, url, updated }),
    personJsonLd(),
    breadcrumbJsonLd(trail),
  ]}
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />
  <h1>About The Founder Playbook</h1>
  <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:.9rem;">Updated {updated}</p>

  <h2>Who writes this</h2>

  <p>
    I am Paulina Xu — <a href={GITHUB_PROFILE_URL}>GitHub</a>{LINKEDIN_URL && (
      <>, <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a></>
    )} — founder of <a href={PARENT_URL}>Agentic Fabriq</a>. I write this playbook because the advice I
    needed as a first-time founder existed in three unhelpful forms: a war story from a
    company nothing like mine, a framework with no numbers in it, or a thread that stopped
    exactly where the hard part started.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the standard here is narrow. Each guide takes one decision a founder actually faces,
    lays out the options genuinely on the table, says what each one costs, and ends with
    something you can act on this week. If a guide cannot do that, it does not ship.
  </p>

  <h2>The editorial standard</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Numbers carry a source.</strong> Where a guide cites a reply rate, a round size,
    a salary band or a conversion benchmark, it links the organisation that published it,
    not a blog that restated it. Benchmarks are reported with their sample, because a
    median across four thousand companies that are nothing like yours is trivia, not
    guidance.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>No number is better than a soft one.</strong> Plenty of founder questions have
    no credible published answer. Where that is the case, the guide says so and gives you
    the reasoning instead of a confident-sounding figure with nothing behind it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Worked examples are labelled.</strong> Where a guide walks through a scenario
    with numbers in it, that scenario is a hypothetical composite and is labelled as one.
    A real company appears by name only where a primary source supports the specific claim
    being made about it. You should never have to guess whether you are reading a fact or
    an illustration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>It stays evergreen on purpose.</strong> Tool pricing, model capability and
    market conditions move faster than anyone can maintain. Where a guide depends on a fact
    with a short shelf life, the fact is isolated and dated rather than woven through the
    argument, so refreshing it is one edit instead of a rewrite.
  </p>

  <h2>What this playbook refuses to do</h2>

  <p>
    There is no newsletter, no email capture, no gate, no analytics and no comment section.
    Nothing here is trying to convert you. If a guide names a tool, it is because the
    decision in front of you is about that tool.
  </p>

  <p>
    One relationship is worth declaring plainly rather than burying. I run an AI
    infrastructure company, so the <a href={withBase('/build/')}>Build</a> pillar touches
    subjects I have commercial interest in. Where a founder topic runs into the engineering
    problem of giving AI agents access to real systems — mailboxes, repositories,
    databases — I link the <a href={AF_PLAYBOOK_URL}>Agent Integration Playbook</a>, which
    is my company's free technical library and the engineering companion to this one. That
    is a cross-reference, not a recommendation to buy anything, and nothing on this site is
    gated behind a product.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>

  <p>
    The site is a static Astro build published from
    <a href={REPO_URL}>a public repository</a> by GitHub Pages. Every guide is a Markdown
    file whose frontmatter is validated at build time, and the build refuses to ship on a
    broken internal link, an orphaned page, a missing social card, a title or description
    outside its length bounds, or a README index that has drifted from the content. The
    review bar is written down in <a href={contributing}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a>; the checks
    themselves are in the repository, so you can read them rather than take my word for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Found an error?</h2>

  <p>
    Open an issue on <a href={ISSUES_URL}>the issue tracker</a>. The most useful report
    names the guide, quotes the claim, and says what the right answer is and where it comes
    from. Corrections to cited numbers get priority over everything else, because a wrong
    number here propagates into somebody's actual decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Licence</h2>

  <p>
    Prose is licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>:
    quote it, translate it, teach from it, keep the attribution. The code in the repository
    — the build, the templates, the checks — is
    <a href="https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0">Apache-2.0</a>, so you can lift
    the whole engine and publish your own library on it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are early and reading this at a bad hour, start with
    <a href={withBase('/sell/')}>founder-led sales</a>. It is the pillar first-time founders
    postpone longest and regret postponing most. If you already have revenue and the
    problem is your own week, start with <a href={withBase('/run/')}>the CEO job</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
